### What does removing all spaces mean?

Removing all spaces from a string produces a new string that keeps every non-space character in its original order while dropping every space character. In Java, strings are immutable, so you cannot modify the original. You create a new result by iterating over each character and appending only the ones that are not spaces.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class SpaceRemover {
    public static String removeAllSpaces(String input) {
        if (input == null) return null;
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (c != ' ') {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

Customers paste account numbers, IFSC codes, and PAN numbers from emails, PDFs, and statements. These pasted values carry hidden spaces, tabs, and sometimes newline characters. A database lookup on "2001 4500" fails even though the human-readable number matches "200145007890". Rajesh at the National Bank of India runs this cleaning step before storage and before any matching query.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.util.SpaceRemover;

public class AccountLookupService {
    public String cleanAndLookup(String rawAccountNumber) {
        String cleaned = SpaceRemover.removeAllSpaces(rawAccountNumber);
        System.out.println("Looking up: '" + cleaned + "'");
        // perform database lookup with cleaned value
        return cleaned;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountLookupService service = new AccountLookupService();
        service.cleanAndLookup("2001 4500 7890");
    }
}
```

```text
Looking up: '200145007890'
```

### How do you remove all whitespace, not just spaces?

The ASCII space character is just one kind of whitespace. Tabs, newlines, and carriage returns are also whitespace. When a customer copies from a spreadsheet, the pasted text may contain tabs. Use `Character.isWhitespace()` to catch all of them.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class WhitespaceRemover {
    public static String removeAllWhitespace(String input) {
        if (input == null) return null;
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (!Character.isWhitespace(c)) {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String pasted = "2001\t4500\n7890";
        System.out.println("Before: '" + pasted.replace("\t", "[TAB]").replace("\n", "[NL]") + "'");
        System.out.println("After:  '" + removeAllWhitespace(pasted) + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Before: '2001[TAB]4500[NL]7890'
After:  '200145007890'
```

### How is trim() different from removing all spaces?

The `trim()` method removes only leading and trailing whitespace. It leaves spaces inside the string untouched. For a display name like " Priya Sharma ", trimming produces "Priya Sharma" which is correct. But for an account number like " 2001 4500 ", trimming produces "2001 4500" which still has the inner space. Full removal is needed for identifiers.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class TrimVsRemoveDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "  Priya Sharma  ";
        String account = "  2001 4500  ";

        System.out.println("Name trimmed:    '" + name.trim() + "'");
        System.out.println("Account trimmed: '" + account.trim() + "'");
        System.out.println("Account cleaned: '" + WhitespaceRemover.removeAllWhitespace(account) + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Name trimmed:    'Priya Sharma'
Account trimmed: '2001 4500'
Account cleaned: '20014500'
```

### What are the alternative approaches?

Java provides several built-in methods. `String.replace(" ", "")` removes only ASCII spaces. `String.replaceAll("\\s", "")` removes all whitespace using a regex but recompiles the pattern on every call. For hot paths, pre-compile the pattern.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class AlternativeApproaches {
    private static final Pattern WHITESPACE = Pattern.compile("\\s");

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "2001 4500\t7890";

        // Approach 1: replace only ASCII space
        System.out.println(input.replace(" ", ""));         // "20014500\t7890" (tab stays)

        // Approach 2: replaceAll with regex (recompiles each call)
        System.out.println(input.replaceAll("\\s", ""));    // "200145007890"

        // Approach 3: pre-compiled pattern (best for hot paths)
        System.out.println(WHITESPACE.matcher(input).replaceAll("")); // "200145007890"
    }
}
```

```text
20014500	7890
200145007890
200145007890
```

### What are the edge cases?

Null input should return null or throw, depending on convention. An empty string returns an empty string. A string of all spaces returns an empty string. A string with no spaces at all returns itself unchanged.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("null:       " + SpaceRemover.removeAllSpaces(null));
        System.out.println("empty:      '" + SpaceRemover.removeAllSpaces("") + "'");
        System.out.println("all spaces: '" + SpaceRemover.removeAllSpaces("   ") + "'");
        System.out.println("no spaces:  '" + SpaceRemover.removeAllSpaces("200145") + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
null:       null
empty:      ''
all spaces: ''
no spaces:  '200145'
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is using `replace` when all whitespace needs removal, since `replace(" ", "")` leaves tabs and newlines. The second mistake is not pre-sizing the StringBuilder, causing repeated internal array growth on large strings. The third mistake is confusing `trim()` with full removal.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = " 2001\t4500 ";

        // WRONG: replace only removes ASCII space, tab stays
        String wrong = input.replace(" ", "");
        System.out.println("Wrong: '" + wrong + "' (tab still present)");

        // CORRECT: remove all whitespace
        String correct = WhitespaceRemover.removeAllWhitespace(input);
        System.out.println("Correct: '" + correct + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: '2001	4500' (tab still present)
Correct: '20014500'
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The loop runs in O(n) time where n is the length of the input string. It uses O(n) extra space for the StringBuilder. Pre-sizing the StringBuilder to the input length avoids internal array resizing, which matters for bulk import of millions of account identifiers at the National Bank of India.

```java
package com.bank.util;

public class PerformanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pre-sized StringBuilder avoids resizing
        String large = "1234 5678 ".repeat(100_000);
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        String result = WhitespaceRemover.removeAllWhitespace(large);
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("Length: " + result.length() + ", Time: " + elapsed / 1_000_000 + "ms");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you remove only duplicate spaces, keeping single spaces between words?
- How would you replace multiple consecutive spaces with a single space?
- What is the difference between `trim()`, `strip()` (Java 11), and removing all spaces?
- How would you handle Unicode whitespace characters like non-breaking space?
